Subject: [PATCH] state the GART aperture semantics the driver actually needs

Patches 0002 and 0003 change two behaviours that HEAD locks in with tests, so
those tests have to say what the hardware says instead. Both are semantic
corrections, not test-chasing:

  Gfx12GartFaultsOutsideTheConfiguredAperture
    -> Gfx12GartPassesThroughAccessesOutsideTheConfiguredAperture

  The aperture says which addresses are translated, not which addresses a
  GART-bound engine may reach. Kernel SDMA rings bind to the one GART address
  space (GpuVm exposes no separate physical handle they could use), and the
  driver points them at VRAM -- rings, indirect buffers, and the page-table
  stores the driver itself issues all land in the frame buffer, outside the
  aperture. A VMID-0 engine addressing the frame buffer is a pass-through, not
  a fault. Faulting it deadlocks amdgpu in kfd_ioctl_acquire_vm with "ring
  sdma0.0 timeout". (The walker's own PTE reads are not at issue: those go
  straight to PhysicalMemoryAccess, below translation.)

  Gfx12GartUsesByteBoundariesAndPtePermissions

  Same change for the one aperture_end + 1 expectation. The byte-boundary and
  permission expectations either side of it are untouched.

  GpuVmRejectsUnusableGartPublications

  Drops the page_table_base = 0x1001 case and adds
  GpuVmAcceptsAGartRootCarryingPdeFlagBits in its place.
  amdgpu_gmc_pd_addr() ORs the PDE control bits into PAGE_TABLE_BASE_ADDR
  before the driver writes it, so a real root arrives as 0x6900005 and is never
  4 KiB aligned. The other four rejections -- empty config, unaligned aperture
  start, inverted range, short range -- still hold.

  The new test also pins the width check the masked validation must keep:
  a root with bits above the physical address width is still refused.

